---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('shopify-email')!;
const path = '/apps/shopify-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/shopify-email');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/shopify-email');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Shopify Email now Shopify Messaging?",
    "answer": "Yes. Shopify Messaging is the current name and covers native campaigns and marketing automations with channel-specific eligibility and setup."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must growing stores graduate after a year?",
    "answer": "No. Stay when required journeys and operating needs are met. Store age and order volume alone do not justify migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is needed for automation?",
    "answer": "Review the current requirements: Basic or higher, Flow for full functionality, appropriate Marketing and Flow app permissions, and setup for additional channels."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does native tooling only send newsletters?",
    "answer": "No. Current documentation includes browse, cart and checkout recovery plus welcome and purchase-related templates. Check the exact template and channel in the store."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does every signup enter the same welcome?",
    "answer": "Do not assume it does. Test the form and checkout subscription routes against the selected template and inspect actual entries."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify customer membership mean marketing permission?",
    "answer": "No. Identity and segment membership are separate from channel permission. Inspect recipient settings, current subscription state and suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare cost?",
    "answer": "Count campaign and automation messages, apply current channel billing terms, and include operator time. Evaluate a replacement against a functioning native baseline."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep native campaigns with another automation sender?",
    "answer": "Yes, if audiences and workflow ownership are explicit. Reconcile scheduled campaigns, stop competing entries and drain or cancel old queues before activation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are recovery percentages promised here?",
    "answer": "No. Staff fixtures test behavior and planning arithmetic uses assumptions. Attributed revenue does not establish incremental lift."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Evaluate Shopify Messaging campaigns and native automations from the store’s actual requirements. Configuration, templates, audiences and channel permissions matter more than a preset graduation date.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Native campaigns and automation scope</h2>
      <p>Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email, brings marketing into Shopify Admin. Native customer audiences and catalog-oriented campaign work are useful when the merchant wants fewer connections to maintain. The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Messaging overview</a> describes campaigns and automations. Native tooling should be evaluated for ongoing operations, with no mandatory graduation date.</p>
      <p>Begin with a requirements sheet: message purpose, entry event, audience, channel, delay, product content, exclusion rule and success measure. Compare the configured native journey with that sheet before evaluating another sender. Store age, monthly orders and an app ranking do not establish a missing capability.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Configuration, staff access and channel readiness</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/requirements">campaign requirements</a> specify a paid active store, Online Store channel and payment provider, with additional channel conditions. The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/requirements-considerations">automation requirements</a> specify Basic or higher, Flow for full functionality, and Marketing plus Flow app permissions for staff. Channel availability must be checked in the actual store.</p>
      <p>Use the desktop configuration for editing and non-abandonment automation work. Record the operator who can edit Messaging and Flow, the sender setup, and the channel activation state. A missing staff permission can prevent a legitimate workflow from being configured; changing suppliers does not diagnose that problem.</p>
      <p>Keep a configuration acceptance record with screenshots or exported settings available to the merchant: store plan, installed Flow app, staff role and active channel. Do not mark a workflow ready simply because a template appears in the menu.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Templates and custom Flow work</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">creation guide</a> distinguishes browse, cart and checkout recovery and documents welcome and post-purchase options. Available channels differ by template. Custom journeys or workflows involving other apps belong in Flow. Inspect the available template and its recipient settings before adapting it.</p>
      <p>For a proposed welcome fixture, subscribe a staff identity through the intended online-store form. Separately create an order with checkout marketing opt-in. Record which action enters the chosen template; do not assume all subscription routes trigger the same journey. Verify the intended buyer/nonbuyer policy before writing a sequence.</p>
      <p>For a custom replenishment requirement, specify the purchased product, expected usage period, repeat-order exclusion and permission state. Demonstrate the necessary data and conditions in Flow. If the native configuration cannot satisfy a required condition, record that precise gap and compare a candidate on the same fixture.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Segments and campaign recipient acceptance</h2>
      <p>Create a proposed audience from the actual customer segment needed for a collection launch. Include staff records that are eligible, recently purchased, outside the intended region and unsubscribed. Review membership before scheduling, then change one record and inspect how the scheduled campaign handles the changed state.</p>
      <p>Customer identity, segment membership and channel permission are separate checks. A tag such as VIP describes a business audience; it does not establish permission to receive every marketing channel. Record how recipient counts are produced and what exclusions apply rather than equating all Shopify customers with subscribers.</p>
      <p>Use this exercise to decide whether native segment editing fits the team. The acceptance criterion is that the operator can explain and reproduce the audience. A manual export or a second sender should solve a demonstrated need rather than being a default step in every campaign.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Catalog, creative and destination checks</h2>
      <p>Native product content reduces the need to maintain a separate merchandising catalog. For a proposed pottery-store campaign, select the actual glaze and variant, then preview the image, price, currency, stock message and destination. Change the product after drafting and inspect what updates before scheduling; do not promise that a message already delivered will refresh.</p>
      <p>Test sold-out and discontinued items, a missing image and a discounted variant. Open the destination as a customer rather than an authenticated administrator. Ensure the page and checkout honor the offer shown in the campaign. Replace unavailable products in scheduled creative and name the owner responsible for that review.</p>
      <p>Preview mobile layouts and plain fallback content. Verify sender identity and links with staff recipients. Keep a campaign approval record covering audience, offer, creative version and planned timing so an Admin convenience does not bypass merchandising review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Browse, cart and checkout are different entries</h2>
      <p>Proposed recovery fixtures should separately cover viewing a product and leaving, adding to cart without checkout, and starting an unfinished checkout. Record which automation receives each case. The distinction matters for eligibility, message content and overlap; three recovery templates should not be treated as interchangeable reminders.</p>
      <p>For each configured path, purchase before the delay expires, then inspect pending and delivered messages. Also test an updated cart, changed variant and repeated event. Record actual suppression and entry behavior. A staff preview validates creative, while a staged journey validates event handling; neither establishes a live recovery rate.</p>
      <p>Assign ownership where native recovery and another app might both respond to the same shopper. The acceptance fixture is one intended sequence for the cohort, with an explicit purchase exclusion. Do not infer lost revenue from a competitor percentage or describe hypothetical recovered orders as observed lift.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Purchase follow-up and channel permission</h2>
      <p>Proposed purchase fixtures: an ordinary product order, a digital gift card, a refund and a second purchase before a delayed message. Define whether each should receive education, a cross-sell or no marketing follow-up. Distinguish the buyer from a gift recipient and inspect the actual customer attached to the entry.</p>
      <p>For permission review, keep staff cases for email subscription, SMS subscription where eligible, and withdrawal while a message is waiting. Check the specific template recipient setting. Checkout email has a documented recipient option that differs from SMS; do not generalize that exception into permission for other channels or purposes.</p>
      <p>If a configured channel becomes unavailable, inspect the automation status and arrange an operator response. An approved email path does not automatically authorize an SMS fallback. Keep the intended channel, recipient evidence and suppression requirement in the handoff.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Volume worksheet and incremental economics</h2>
      <p>Use current Shopify billing documentation for planned campaigns and automations, including separate channel rates and any allowance. The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/requirements-considerations">requirements and billing guidance</a> distinguishes checkout-recovery email from other automation volume. Record expected eligible recipients and messages per journey; customer count alone is not message volume.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical pottery-store budget: 420 eligible contacts receiving four campaigns implies 1,680 campaign sends before automation volume. Twelve genuinely additional orders at $56 and an assumed 50% contribution margin produce $336. After $60 messaging expense and $120 operating labor, contribution is $156. With no additional orders, those expenses produce a $180 loss. These are planning inputs, not a supplier price quote or measured campaign outcome.</p>
      <p>When considering a replacement, subtract its incremental license, message, integration and ongoing labor cost from the measured improvement over a functioning native baseline. Attributed revenue can include purchases that would have happened anyway. Use comparable cohorts where feasible and report attribution limits rather than promising a payback week.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Controlled rollout and queue acceptance</h2>
      <p>Save the configured workflow and recipient settings before changing a template or Flow condition. Start with staff fixtures, then a bounded eligible cohort. Record entry, wait, send, purchase and suppression decisions so failures can be distinguished from missing permission or a channel that is not active.</p>
      <p>If introducing another sender, stop new native entries for the selected cohort and cancel or drain pending messages before replacement activation. Deduplicate replayed events and recheck current purchase and permission state at send time. Permanent use of multiple senders needs explicit audience and workflow boundaries, including campaigns that can collide with automations.</p>
      <p>Rollback should specify who pauses the new path, what happens to queued recipients and which native configuration can be restored. Do not automatically restart every contact at step one. Avoid switching providers during a trading peak unless a concrete problem requires it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Operating review and the decision to stay</h2>
      <p>Review draft or inactive automations, staff access changes, segment membership and scheduled creative as routine operations. After catalog changes, inspect linked products; after permission or channel changes, inspect pending journeys. Compare billing with the planned message ledger rather than using a free allowance as the whole business case.</p>
      <p>Stay with native Messaging when the required audience, content, timing, channel and reporting work is demonstrably supported and manageable. Compare another platform when a specific unmet requirement or operating burden remains after configuration. There is no 6–12 month deadline or 400–500 order threshold.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/apps/privy">Privy review</a> addresses display design, identity and offer handoff when capture itself needs work. The <a href="/apps/tinyemail">tinyEmail review</a> evaluates a separate campaign workspace. The <a href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email">Sequenzy</a>, <a href="/compare/omnisend-vs-shopify-email">Omnisend</a> and <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email">Klaviyo comparisons</a> should be used to demonstrate the same required workflow, rather than to assume native automation is absent.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="link" href="/apps">All Shopify email apps →</a>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Shopify Email FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
